 see any error messages or exceptions that occurred during the pod's runtime. \n\nWait, the user already used 'kubectl get events' and saw some PVC issues. But the PVCs might be related to the MongoDB services. For example, the 'mongodb-profile' pod couldn't schedule because the PVC wasn't found. But the pod is still running, so maybe the PVC was created later? Or maybe there's a misconfiguration in the PVC claims. \n\nAlternatively, the 'geo' pod had a back-off restart. The event mentioned a container failure. Checking the logs for the 'geo' pod might show why the container crashed. Also, the 'rate' pod had two restarts. Looking into its logs could indicate if there's an application error or resource issue. \n\nSo, the next logical step is to check the logs of the pods that have restarts. The user should use 'kubectl logs' on those specific pods to get more details. For example, 'kubectl logs rate-6fff88f6f5-6mxgg' and 'kubectl logs user-6548d868cd-67rwg' to see if there are any errors. Also, the 'geo' pod's logs might show why it's restarting. \n\nBy examining the logs, the user can identify if there are specific errors like connection issues, missing dependencies, or application crashes. This would help pinpoint the root cause of the restarts and any potential faults in the microservices.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nreasoning: The events show PVC scheduling issues for `mongodb-profile` and `mongodb-rate`, but the pods are still running. To investigate why specific pods like `rate-6fff88f6f5-6mxgg` and `user-6548d868cd-67rwg` are restarting, I need to check their container logs for errors. Running `kubectl logs` on these pods will reveal application-level issues, such as crashes, missing dependencies, or misconfigurations. This is critical for identifying the root cause of the restarts.", additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 568, 'prom