 were already checked, and since there were no errors, the issue might be resource limits. But without metrics, they can\'t confirm CPU or memory usage. \n\nSo the next step is to check the pod\'s describe output for any clues. If that\'s not possible, maybe check the deployment\'s resources. But the user can\'t use `kubectl top`, so they might need to look at the deployment\'s YAML to see if resource limits are set. \n\nWait, the user has the `exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd` tool. They can run `kubectl describe pod` to get events and conditions. Let me check the previous logs. The user tried `kubectl logs` and saw no errors, but the pod had restarts. \n\nSo the next logical step is to run `kubectl describe pod` on the \'rate\' pod to check for any events or conditions that might explain the restarts. That should be the next tool call.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nreasoning: The `kubectl top pod` command failed due to permission issues, so I cannot retrieve resource metrics (CPU/memory) for the pod. To investigate restarts further, I need to check the pod\'s detailed status, including events, conditions, and restart reasons, using `kubectl describe pod`. This will reveal if the restarts are due to OOM, probe failures, or other infrastructure-level issues. \n\n<tool_call>\n{"name": "exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd", "arguments": {"command": "kubectl describe pod rate-6fff88f6f5-6mxgg -n hotel-reservation"}}\n</tool_call>', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 561, 'prompt_tokens': 11162, 'total_tokens': 11723, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc1d-9589-7872-89ae-4d460f773262-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 11162, 'output_tokens': 561, 'total_tok