  Port:          8084/TCP\n    Host Port:     0/TCP\n    Command:\n      rate\n    State:          Running\n      Started:      Tue, 17 Mar 2026 14:03:11 +0000\n    Last State:     Terminated\n      Reason:       Error\n      Exit Code:    2\n      Started:      Tue, 17 Mar 2026 14:02:41 +0000\n      Finished:     Tue, 17 Mar 2026 14:02:54 +0000\n    Ready:          True\n    Restart Count:  2\n    Limits:\n      cpu:  1\n    Requests:\n      cpu:  100m\n    Environment:\n      JAEGER_SAMPLE_RATIO:  1\n    Mounts:\n      /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount from kube-api-access-kkx2k (ro)\nConditions:\n  Type                        Status\n  PodReadyToStartContainers   True \n  Initialized                 True \n  Ready                       True \n  ContainersReady             True \n  PodScheduled                True \nVolumes:\n  kube-api-access-kkx2k:\n    Type:                    Projected (a volume that contains injected data from multiple sources)\n    TokenExpirationSeconds:  3607\n    ConfigMapName:           kube-root-ca.crt\n    Optional:                false\n    DownwardAPI:             true\nQoS Class:                   Burstable\nNode-Selectors:              <none>\nTolerations:                 node.kubernetes.io/not-ready:NoExecute op=Exists for 300s\n                             node.kubernetes.io/unreachable:NoExecute op=Exists for 300s\nEvents:\n  Type     Reason     Age                    From               Message\n  ----     ------     ----                   ----               -------\n  Normal   Scheduled  3m36s                  default-scheduler  Successfully assigned hotel-reservation/rate-6fff88f6f5-6mxgg to node1.htliang2-agent.insai-pg0.apt.emulab.net\n  Warning  BackOff    2m36s (x2 over 2m39s)  kubelet            spec.containers{hotel-reserv-rate}: Back-off restarting failed container hotel-reserv-rate in pod rate-6fff88f6f5-6mxgg_hotel-reservation(47fc410f-c0a0-49cb-aef6-5c5f821e1f58)\n  Normal   Pulled     2m25s (x3 over 3m23s)  kubelet            spec.containers{hotel-reserv-rate}: Container image "yinfangchen/hotelreservation:latest" already present on machine\n  Normal   Created    2m24s (x3 over 3m16s)  kubelet            spec.containers{hotel-reserv-rate}: Created container: hotel-reserv-rate\n  Normal   Started    2m23s (x3 over 3m14s)  kubelet            spec.containers{hotel-reserv-rate}: Started container hotel-reserv-rate\n', id='3f8bf231-8caf-433f-b8bf-2ad4f3a4431e', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-8cae53eb914e7fec'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='90c47caf-89d9-4ee4-8e53-ea77a115ddbe'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose why the \'rate\' pod is restarting. From the previous steps, they checked the logs and found no errors, but the pod has two restarts. Then they used \'kubectl describe pod\' and saw that the container exited with code 2, which usually indicates an error. The events show a BackOff warning, meaning the kubelet is restarting the container after failures.\n\nNow, the next step is to figure out why the container is exiting with code 2. Since the logs didn\'t show any issues, maybe the problem is related to the container\'s entry point or the command being executed. The \'rate\' pod\'s container is running the \'rate\' command, which might be failing for some reason. \n\nLooking at the pod\'s spec, the container\'s command is just \'rate\', which is probably a binary or script. If that command is not found or fails, it would exit with code 2. But why isn\'t the log showing that? Maybe the logs are not capturing the error, or the container is exiting too quickly. \n\nAnother possibility is that the container\'s entry point is misconfigured. The user should check the container\'s image to see if the \'rate\' command is correctly installed and executable. Also, checking the pod\'s resource limits might help—maybe the container is getting OOM killed, but the logs don\'t show that. \n\nWait, the \'kubectl describe pod\' output shows the container\'s image is \'yinfangchen/hotelreservation:latest\'. The user might need to inspect the image to see if the \'rate\' command is present. Alternatively, maybe there\'s a missing dependency in the image that\'s causing the command to fail. \n\nSince the user can\'t use \'kubectl top pod\' due to permissions, they might need to check the pod\'s events again or look into the container\'s filesystem. But with the current tools, maybe the next step is to check the pod\'s configuration, like the command and args, to ensure they\'re correct. \n\nAlternatively, the user could check the pod\'s status again for any other events or conditions that might indicate the cause. But since the describe output already shows the container\'s exit code and the BackOff events, the next logical step is to verify the container\'s command and image. \n\nWait, the user has the \'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd\' tool. They could try to exec into the pod to check the container\'s processes or files, but since it\'s a read-only command, maybe they can\'t. Alternatively, they could check the pod\'s YAML definition to see the command and args. \n\nSo, the next tool call should be to get the pod\'s YAML or JSON to inspect the container\'s command and args. That way, they can confirm if the \'rate\' command is correctly specified. If the command is wrong, that\'s the root cause. \n\nAlternatively, if the command is correct, maybe there\'s an issue with the image. The user could check the image\'s manifest or try to run the image locally to see if the \'rate\' command works. But with the current tools, maybe they can\'t do that. \n\nSo, the best next step is to retrieve the pod\'s specification to check the container\'s command and args. That would help confirm if the command is correctly set. If it\'s not, that\'s the issue. If it is, then maybe there\'s a problem with the image or runtime environment.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nreasoning: The `rate` pod\'s container exited with code 2 and is restarting due to a "BackOff" error. Since logs show no application errors, the issue might be with the container\'s entry point or command. To verify, I need to inspect the pod\'s YAML specification to confirm the container\'s `command` and `args` are correctly defined. This will ensure the `rate` binary is properly invoked and no misconfiguration exists. \n\n<tool_call>\n{"name": "exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd", "arguments": {"command": "kubectl get pod rate-6fff88f6f5-6mxgg -n hotel-reservation -o yaml"}}\n</tool_call>', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 863, 'prompt_tokens': 12559, 'total_tokens': 13422, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc1d-c478-7f23-a25a-df5775528beb-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 12559, 'output_tokens': 863, 'total_tokens': 13422}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='cc76da6b-a540-4ddc-829e-b1ba601bb0dd'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user is trying to diagnose why the 'rate' pod is restarting. They've already checked the logs and found no errors, and the describe pod output shows the container exited with code 2 and is being restarted due to a BackOff. The next step was to get the pod's YAML to check the command and args.\n\nSo, the user ran `kubectl get pod rate-6fff88f6f5-6mxgg -n hotel-reservation -o yaml` to get the detailed specification. Now, they need to analyze the YAML to see if the container's command is correctly set. If the command is wrong, like missing arguments or incorrect binary paths, that could cause the container to exit with code 2. Also, checking the resources (CPU, memory) and any liveness/readiness probes might help. But since the user is focusing on the command, they should look for the 'command' field under the container specs. If the command isn't properly defined, that could be the root cause. Once they find the YAML, they can verify if the 'rate' binary is being called correctly with the right arguments. If everything looks good there, maybe other issues like resource limits or probes are the problem, but the initial step is to check the command.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get pod rate-6fff88f6f5-6mxgg -n hotel-reservation -o yaml"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-9b6a4a862efc72f3', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 318, 'prompt_tokens': 13614, 'total_tokens': 13932, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc1d-f041-7560-bf30-4a1f7a273c88-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get pod rate-6fff88f6f5-6mxgg -n hotel-reservation -o yaml'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-9b6a4a862efc72f3', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 13614, 'output_tokens': 318, 'total_tokens': 13932}), ToolMessage(content='apiVersion: v1\nkind: Pod\nmetadata:\n  annotations:\n    kompose.cmd: kompose convert\n    kompose.version: 1.22.0 (955b78124)\n    sidecar.istio.io/statsInclusionPrefixes: cluster.outbound,cluster_manager,listener_manager,http_mixer_filter,tcp_mixer_filter,server,cluster.xds-grp,listener,connection_manager\n    sidecar.istio.io/statsInclusionR