ample, if the binary is \'main\', then the command should be ["main"]. However, without knowing the exact binary name, this is a guess. \n\nAlternatively, the user could check the metrics or logs from Grafana or Prometheus to see if the pod is healthy. But the available tools include get_metrics, which could help. However, the user hasn\'t used that yet. \n\nWait, the user\'s last action was to restart the deployment. Now, they need to check if the pod is running correctly. They could use \'kubectl get pods\' to check the status. If the pod is still crashing, then the issue persists. \n\nBut the user can\'t use \'kubectl logs\' because the pod might not be running. So, the next step is to check the pod\'s status and ensure it\'s in a running state. If it\'s still crashing, then the command or image is still incorrect. \n\nSo, the next tool call should be to check the pod\'s status and logs again. However, the previous attempt to get logs failed because the pod wasn\'t found. Maybe the pod is now running, so they should check again. \n\nAlternatively, the user could check the deployment\'s rollout status to see if it\'s successful. If the deployment is updated, the pod should be recreated. \n\nIn summary, the next step is to verify the pod\'s current status and ensure the deployment is successful. If the pod is still not running, then further adjustments to the container\'s command or image are needed.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_kubectl_cmd_safely\nreasoning: After restarting the deployment, I need to verify if the pod is now running successfully. If the issue persists, the root cause may still be the misconfigured container command. I will check the pod\'s current status and logs to confirm if the "rate" command is correctly recognized or if further adjustments are needed.\n\ntool_call: {"command": "kubectl get pods -n hotel-reservation"}', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': Usage