e binary is \'main\', then the command should be ["main"]. However, without knowing the exact binary name, this is a guess. \n\nAlternatively, the user could check the metrics or logs from Grafana or Prometheus to see if the pod is healthy. But the available tools include get_metrics, which could help. However, the user hasn\'t used that yet. \n\nWait, the user\'s last action was to restart the deployment. Now, they need to check if the pod is running correctly. They could use \'kubectl get pods\' to check the status. If the pod is still crashing, then the issue persists. \n\nBut the user can\'t use \'kubectl logs\' because the pod might not be running. So, the next step is to check the pod\'s status and ensure it\'s in a running state. If it\'s still crashing, then the command or image is still incorrect. \n\nSo, the next tool call should be to check the pod\'s status and logs again. However, the previous attempt to get logs failed because the pod wasn\'t found. Maybe the pod is now running, so they should check again. \n\nAlternatively, the user could check the deployment\'s rollout status to see if it\'s successful. If the deployment is updated, the pod should be recreated. \n\nIn summary, the next step is to verify the pod\'s current status and ensure the deployment is successful. If the pod is still not running, then further adjustments to the container\'s command or image are needed.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_kubectl_cmd_safely\nreasoning: After restarting the deployment, I need to verify if the pod is now running successfully. If the issue persists, the root cause may still be the misconfigured container command. I will check the pod\'s current status and logs to confirm if the "rate" command is correctly recognized or if further adjustments are needed.\n\ntool_call: {"command": "kubectl get pods -n hotel-reservation"}', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 802, 'prompt_tokens': 3648, 'total_tokens': 4450, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc1f-4032-73a1-a197-8db9a788e5c8-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 3648, 'output_tokens': 802, 'total_tokens': 4450}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='b4219ff4-9a8e-4866-9294-63f956365766'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let me try to figure out what\'s going on here. The user is dealing with a Kubernetes cluster where the \'rate\' pod keeps restarting. The initial logs showed exit code 2, which usually means a command not found. The deployment\'s YAML showed the container\'s command is set to ["rate"], which might be the issue.\n\nFirst, the user tried to get the pod\'s YAML and logs, but the pod wasn\'t found, so they checked the deployment. The deployment\'s pod template had the command set to ["rate"], which might be incorrect. The user then tried updating the image and restarting the deployment, but that didn\'t resolve the issue.\n\nNow, after restarting the deployment, the user needs to check the current pod status. If the pod is still not running or keeps crashing, the problem is likely the command. The next step is to check the pod\'s logs again to see if the same error persists. If the logs show the same exit code, the command might be wrong. Alternatively, maybe the image doesn\'t have the \'rate\' binary, so the correct entry point or command needs to be set. The user should also verify the container\'s entry point in the Dockerfile or image configuration. If all else fails, they might need to adjust the command to the correct binary name or use the image\'s default entry point.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl logs rate-6fff88f6f5-66666 -n hotel-reservation"}', 'name': 'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-84c88c0e05c85ac9', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 324, 'prompt_tokens': 4765, 'total_tokens': 5089, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc1f-658f-74f1-9db4-f976b9a90e9d-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl logs rate-6fff88f6f5-66666 -n hotel-reservation'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-84c88c0e05c85ac9', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 4765, 'output_tokens': 324, 'total_tokens': 5089}), ToolMessage(content='Command Rejected: Error executing kubectl command:\nerror: error from server (NotFound): pods "rate-6fff88f6f5-66666" not found in namespace "hotel-reservation"\n', id='c698d41a-2728-4130-af16-9494170d5069', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-84c88c0e05c85ac9'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='b2592e6b-4d1c-4c6e-8ba9-39036d04afeb')]]
