Next, generate the tool call for `wait_tool` to pause execution for 30 seconds. - base_agent.py:llm_thinking_step:68 [Full Prompt = [SystemMessage(content='Mitigate the identified faults in an IT incident.  Some or none of the microservices have faults.  Get all the pods and deployments to figure out what kind of services are running in the cluster if you don\'t know what the services are. You should carefully identify the whether the faults are present and if they are, what is the root cause of the fault. You can stop mitigation once you\'ve fixed all the faults. \nGo as deep as you can into what is causing the issue, and mitigate the fault.\nYour instructions to the tools must be clear and concise. Your queries to tools need to be single turn.\nRemember to check these, and remember this information: ## Workloads (Applications) - **Pod**: The smallest deployable unit in Kubernetes, representing a single instance of a running application. Can contain one or more tightly coupled containers. - **ReplicaSet**: Ensures that a specified number of pod replicas are running at all times. Often managed indirectly through Deployments. - **Deployment**: Manages the deployment and lifecycle of applications. Provides declarative updates for Pods and ReplicaSets. - **StatefulSet**: Manages stateful applications with unique pod identities and stable storage. Used for workloads like databases. - **DaemonSet**: Ensures that a copy of a specific pod runs on every node in the cluster. Useful for node monitoring agents, log collectors, etc. - **Job**: Manages batch processing tasks that are expected to complete successfully. Ensures pods run to completion. - **CronJob**: Schedules jobs to run at specified times or intervals (similar to cron in Linux).\n## Networking - **Service**: Provides a stable network endpoint for accessing a group of pods. Types: ClusterIP, NodePort, LoadBalancer, and ExternalName. - **Ingress**: Manages external HTTP(S) access to services in the cluster. Supports routing and load balancing for HTTP(S) traffic. - **NetworkPolicy**: Defines rules for network communication between pods and other entities. Used for security and traffic control.\n## Storage - **PersistentVolume (PV)**: Represents a piece of storage in the cluster, provisioned by an administrator or dynamically. - **PersistentVolumeClaim (PVC)**: Represents a request for storage by a user. Binds to a PersistentVolume. - **StorageClass**: Defines different storage tiers or backends for dynamic provisioning of PersistentVolumes. - **ConfigMap**: Stores configuration data as key-value pairs for applications. - **Secret**: Stores sensitive data like passwords, tokens, or keys in an encrypted format.\n## Configuration and Metadata - **Namespace**: Logical partitioning of resources within the cluster for isolation and organization. - **ConfigMap**: Provides non-sensitive configuration data in key-value format. - **Secret**: Stores sensitive configuration data securely. - **ResourceQuota**: Restricts resource usage (e.g., CPU, memory) within a namespace. - **LimitRange**: Enforces minimum and maximum resource limits for containers in a namespace.\n## Cluster Management - **Node**: Represents a worker machine in the cluster (virtual or physical). Runs pods and is managed by the control plane. - **ClusterRole and Role**: Define permissions for resources at the cluster or namespace level. - **ClusterRoleBinding and RoleBinding**: Bind roles to users or groups for authorization. - **ServiceAccount**: Associates processes in pods with permissions for accessing the Kubernetes API.\nAn example procedure to remediate the faults: 1) Formulate a remediation plan with a list of actionable steps. 2) Execute the plan, one step at a time. 3) Check if the plan execution worked as you desired in the IT environment. 4) If not, you can either call wait_tool to wait for it to take effect or take other actions. 5) Otherwise, continue the plan and execution process until you call submit_tool as you believe the application has become healthy.\nThe following is a detailed description of your tasks.\n1) mitigation: Mitigate the identified faults in an IT incident with the provided tools. You can submit an empty dict "ans" with the submit_tool as this task is not graded over your answer but the final result of the mitigation; therefore, you have to make sure the application has become healthy before you call submit_tool.\n', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='7fd1a98e-b066-4fff-a75a-d55b97c8a9fe'), HumanMessage(content='You will be working this application:\n\nHotel Reservation\n\nHere are some descriptions about the application:\n\nA hotel reservation application built with Go and gRPC, providing backend in-memory and persistent databases, a recommender system for hotel recommendations, and a functionality to place reservations.\n\nIt belongs to this namespace:\n\nhotel-reservation\n\nThe following is the information of faults identified by a diagnosis agent in the app:\n\n<think>\nOkay, let me try to figure out what\'s going on here. The user is dealing with a Kubernetes cluster running a hotel reservation application. They\'ve identified some pods that are restarting, specifically the \'rate\' pod. The logs for the \'rate\' pod don\'t show any application-level errors, but the pod keeps restarting with exit code 2.\n\nFirst, they checked the pod\'s YAML configuration using `kubectl get pod ... -o yaml`. The container\'s command is set to ["rate"]. Exit code 2 usually means a command not found or a syntax error. So the problem might be that the \'rate\' command isn\'t available in the container\'s environment.\n\nThe container image is yinfangchen/hotelreservation:latest. Maybe the image doesn\'t have the \'rate\' binary installed or it\'s named differently. The user might need to check the container\'s entry point or the command arguments. The YAML shows the command is set to ["rate"], but perhaps the actual entry point is different, or the binary is named something else like \'rate-service\' or \'main\'.\n\nAnother angle: the \'rate\' command might be a Go binary that\'s not properly built or the entry point isn\'t set correctly. The logs show the application is starting up, connecting to the database, and registering with Consul, but then it exits. The exit code 2 is a bit ambiguous, but in some systems, it can indicate a command not found.\n\nSince the user can\'t run `kubectl exec` to check the container\'s filesystem, they have to rely on the existing information. The YAML shows the command is set to ["rate"], but maybe the actual entry point is different. For example, if the Dockerfile uses `CMD ["rate"]` but the binary is actually named \'rate-service\', then the command would fail.\n\nThe root cause is likely a misconfiguration in the container\'s command or entry point, leading to the \'rate\' command not being found, hence exit code 2. The user should check the container image\'s entry point and ensure the command is correct. Since they can\'t do that here, the best they can do is submit the answer based on the available data.\n</think>\n\nThe root cause of the fault is an invalid or missing command in the `rate` pod\'s container configuration. The container\'s `command` field is set to `["rate"]`, but the exit code 2 indicates the command is not recognized or improperly configured, leading to repeated restarts. This suggests a misconfiguration in the container\'s entry point or missing required arguments for the `rate` binary.\n\nIn each round, there is a thinking stage. In the thinking stage, you are given a list of tools. Think about what you want to call. Return your tool choice and the reasoning behind.\nWhen choosing the tool, refer to the tool by its name.\nThen, there is a tool-call stage, where you make a tool_call consistent with your explanation.\nYou can run up to 20 rounds to finish the tasks.\nIf you call submit_tool in tool-call stage, the process will end immediately.\nIf you exceed this limitation, the system will force you to make a submission.\nYou will begin by analyzing the service\'s state and telemetry with the tools.\n\n\nThe result from the last attempt of mitigation is as follows:\n\n[False, [<clients.stratus.weak_oracles.base_oracle.OracleResult object at 0x7900188cb320>]]\n\nThere are some reflections from the previous run:\n\n<think>\nOkay, let\'s start by going through the conversation step by step. The user is dealing with a Kubernetes cluster where the \'rate\' pod keeps restarting with exit code 2. The LLM is trying to figure out the root cause and resolve it.\n\nFirst, the user provides context about the application and the fault. The LLM\'s initial response is to use the exec_kubectl_cmd_safely tool to get the pod\'s YAML. The response shows the container\'s command is set to ["rate"], which might be the issue. Exit code 2 usually means a command not found, so the LLM correctly identifies that the \'rate\' command might be missing or misconfigured.\n\nNext, the LLM tries to get the pod\'s logs but gets a "Not Found" error. This suggests the pod was deleted or not running. The LLM then checks the deployments and finds that the \'rate\' deployment has zero replicas, which explains why the pod isn\'t found. They then describe the deployment to get more details, which shows the command is still ["rate"].\n\nThe LLM then attempts to update the deployment\'s image and restart it, but the command format is incorrect, leading to an error. After splitting the commands, they successfully restart the deployment. Checking the pods shows a new pod running, and the logs indicate everything is initializing properly without errors. The LLM concludes the issue is resolved and submits the answer.\n\nNow, identifying what worked: The LLM correctly identified the misconfigured command in the deployment, updated the image, and restarted the deployment. The logs after the restart showed no errors, confirming the fix. The LLM also used the right tools step by step to diagnose and resolve the issue.\n\nWhat didn\'t work: The initial attempt to combine multiple commands in one call failed due to syntax issues. The LLM had to split the commands into separate steps. Also, the user had to repeatedly check pod statuses and logs, which could have been more efficient with better error handling or more detailed initial diagnostics.\n</think>\n\n### What Worked\n- The LLM accurately identified the root cause of the pod restarts by analyzing the container\'s command configuration: “The container\'s command is set to ["rate"], but the exit code 2 indicates the command is not recognized or improperly configured.”\n- The LLM successfully resolved the issue by updating the deployment and restarting it, leading to a stable pod with no restarts: “The fault in the rate pod has been resolved. The pod is running stably with no restarts, and logs show successful initialization of database and memcached connections.”\n- The LLM systematically used the `exec_kubectl_cmd_safely` tool to inspect and modify the deployment, ensuring each step was executed sequentially: “kubectl set image deployment/rate hotel-reserv-rate=yinfangchen/hotelreservation:latest -n hotel-reservation && kubectl rollout restart deployment/rate -n hotel-reservation”.\n\n### What Didn’t Work\n- The LLM initially failed to handle combined command syntax correctly, leading to a rejected tool call: “Command Rejected (ValueError): Unsupported operator kind: list” when using `&&` to chain commands.\n- The LLM struggled with retrieving logs for a specific pod due to incorrect pod names, requiring multiple retries to find the correct pod name: “Command Rejected: Error executing kubectl command: error: error from server (NotFound): pods "rate-6fff88f6f5-66666" not found in namespace "hotel-reservation"”.\n- The LLM did not immediately recognize the need to check the container\'s entry point or binary name, leading to unnecessary steps before confirming the fix: “The exit code 2 suggests a command not found or syntax error, but the LLM initially focused on image updates rather than verifying the command’s validity.”\n\nNext, use the provided tools to mitigate the faults.\nIt is a good habit to verify the information of faults first before you take any actions for mitigation.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='c066341e-834a-4ae1-bbef-8c91a80924d1'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Here are all the tools you can use:\ntool name: wait_tool\n\ntool descriptions Use this tool to wait for you action to take effect. The upper limit is 120 seconds. \n    Any value above 120 seconds will be truncated to 120 seconds. If you call this tool\n    along with other tools in your tool_calls list, this tool will be scheduled to the \n    last for execution.\n\n    Args:\n        seconds (int): Number of seconds to wait.\n\n\ntool name: get_traces\n\ntool description: Get Jaeger traces for a given service in the last n minutes.\n\n    Args:\n        service (str): The name of the service for which to retrieve trace data.\n        last_n_minutes (int): The time range (in minutes) to look back from the current time.\n\n\ntool name: get_services\n\ntool description: Retrieve the list of service names from the Grafana instance.\n\n    Args:\n\n    Returns:\n        List[str]: A list of service names available in Grafana.\n\n\ntool name: get_operations\n\ntool description: Query available operations for a specific service from the Grafana instance.\n\n    Args:\n        service (str): The name of the service whose operations should be retrieved.\n\n    Returns:\n        List[str]: A list of operation names associated with the specified service.\n\n\ntool name: get_dependency_graph\n\ntool description: Get service dependency graph from Jaeger\'s native dependencies API.\n    Args:\n        last_n_minutes (int): The time range (in minutes) to look back from the current time.\n    Returns:\n        str: JSON object representing the dependency graph.\n\n\ntool name: get_metrics\n\ntool description: Query real-time metrics data from the Prometheus instance.\n\n    Args:\n        query (str): A Prometheus Query Language (PromQL) expression used to fetch metric values.\n\n    Returns:\n        dict: The raw Prometheus response containing metric results, including timestamps, values, and labels.\n\n\ntool name: exec_kubectl_cmd_safely\n\ntool description: This is a tool used to safely execute kubectl commands. Whatever commands you executed will be stored in a rollback \nmechanism which later is used to roll back the cluster state in case your current attempt fails.\n\n    Args:\n        command (str): The kubectl command you would like to execute, must start with "kubectl"\n        INTERACTIVE COMMANDS ARE NOT SUPPORTED, e.g. "kubectl edit" \n        INTERACTIVE FLAGS ARE NOT SUPPORTED, e.g., "kubectl logs -f"\n\n\ntool name: submit_tool\n\ntool description: The tool to submit benchmark results\n    \n    Args:\n        ans (str): the answer you would like to submit to the benchmark\n\n\nChoose a tool from the list and output the tool name. Justify your tool choice. In the next step, you will generate a tool call for this tool', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='9f08cda3-ea06-413b-9bf0-b23cf7347df6'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is dealing with a hotel reservation application in Kubernetes where the \'rate\' pod keeps restarting. The previous attempts showed that the pod\'s container command was set to ["rate"], which caused exit code 2. The LLM tried updating the deployment image and restarting it, but there were some issues with command syntax.\n\nFirst, I need to figure out what the next step should be. The user has a list of tools available. The goal is to mitigate the faults, so I should check if the current state of the cluster is resolved. The last attempt mentioned that the pod is running stably now, but maybe I should verify that.\n\nLooking at the tools, \'get_metrics\' could be useful to check the pod\'s status and metrics. Alternatively, \'get_traces\' or \'get_dependency_graph\' might help, but since the issue is about pod restarts, checking metrics like pod restart counts would be more direct. \n\nWait, the user already tried using \'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely\' to update the deployment. The last response indicated that the pod is now stable. But to confirm, maybe using \'get_metrics\' to check the restart count would be a good idea. If the restart count is zero, then the issue is resolved. \n\nAlternatively, using \'get_services\' or \'get_operations\' from Grafana might not be necessary here. The main thing is to ensure that the pod is running without issues. Since the previous steps already addressed the command configuration, the next logical step is to confirm the resolution by checking metrics. \n\nSo, the best tool here is \'get_metrics\' with a query to check the pod\'s restart count. That way, we can verify if the problem is truly resolved. If the restart count is zero, then the mitigation is successful. If not, further actions might be needed. \n\nAnother option is to use \'wait_tool\' to wait for a certain period and then check again, but that\'s less efficient. Using \'get_metrics\' directly provides the necessary data. So, I\'ll choose \'get_metrics\' with the appropriate PromQL query.\n</think>\n\ntool name: get_metrics\n\nJustification: To verify if the fault in the \'rate\' pod has been resolved, we need to check real-time metrics such as pod restart counts or error rates. This will confirm whether the pod is running stably without restarts, ensuring the mitigation was successful. The query will target metrics related to pod restarts or application errors for the \'rate\' service. \n\nNext, generate the tool call for `get_metrics` with a PromQL query to check pod restarts.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 539, 'prompt_tokens': 3083, 'total_tokens': 3622, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc20-fe46-7562-a11e-cda3ef487461-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 3083, 'output_tokens': 539, 'total_tokens': 3622}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='9b2aee34-ecab-486e-863c-470f5acb2207'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user wants to check if the 'rate' pod is stable after mitigation. They used the get_metrics tool to query pod restarts. The PromQL query should look for restart counts. The correct query is probably something like `sum by (pod) (count_over_time(kube_pod_container_status_restarts_total[5m]))` to get the total restarts over the last 5 minutes. That way, if the value is zero, the pod is stable. I'll format the tool call with this query.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"query": "sum by (pod) (count_over_time(kube_pod_container_status_restarts_total[5m]))"}', 'name': 'get_metrics'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-b857fa66c91108b0', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 155, 'prompt_tokens': 4211, 'total_tokens': 4366, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc21-17ab-7a01-a837-84530f01925c-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'get_metrics', 'args': {'query': 'sum by (pod) (count_over_time(kube_pod_container_status_restarts_total[5m]))'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-b857fa66c91108b0', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_cal